The plotted result is as shown below. 
![alt text](research_images/opt_cwnd_66_and_reno.png) 
![alt text](research_images/opt_cwnd_66_and_reno_time.png)

As it can be seen, although the optimized CWND is faster than the nornaml TCP Reno, it is not a lot faster. The difference is constantly around 0.5 seconds, which is different than 33% from the simulation. The biggest caused of this may be due to "large TBF queue size". As stated in the report, **"When the queue sizes are large, default TCP New Reno and TCP Cubic can match the algorithms' performance because the buffer can accommodate the size of the data that can fit on the link. The queue never empties, allowing the sender to continuously utilize the outgoing link"**. In the report, it also states that **"increasing the burst size does not influence throughput under the optimal cwnd algorithms"**

- small TBF queue: cwnd keep increasing, buffer cannot accommodate, packets get dropped, performance decreases (TCP detects the drop and lowers the CWND size)
- big TBF queue: cwnd keep increasing, buffer CAN accommodate

![alt text](research_images/image1.png)
![alt text](research_images/image4.png) 

Thus, the best thing to do next is to change the buffer size to compare the performance. It's quite funny as for now, the queue size is 50000 bytes. And in the report it says that...
![alt text](research_images/q_smaller_bdp.png) 
**"Once the queue size reaches the BDP, the improvement drops to zero and remains at that level for larger queue sizes"**. In our case, <ins>we have been testing with a queue size of BDP * 2 or BDP, that is 50000 bytes</ins>.


In addition, think about the application of this research (like under what condition will we have a hardware that has TBF with small queue size) and whether it is possible to demonstrate it. 


# 1Gb with TCP Reno and OPTIMAL_CWND[36], RTT[0.4], TBF_QUEUE[5840 bytes (4p)]
From the graph, <ins>it seems when the burst size is extremely big (500,000 <), even for small TBF queue size, there is not much improvement</ins> (this wasn't explained in the report? but I think the reason why this happens is because, when the burst size is big, it can contain more token. During the time when the queue size is small, some packets will be dropped, and TCP reno will gradually start increasing the cwnd again. By this moment, the token bucket will be filled with so many tokens such that it is able to suddenly increase the throughput, eventually reaching the same performance as the optimal CWND)


![alt text](research_images/image3.png) 


## Testing with the case where queue_size < BDD, burst_size == 50000
Concluding all above, in this experiment, we will try with burst size of 50000, and TBF queue size of 3000 bytes (so 2 packets). The new optimal cwnd will be: (50000 + 5840 - 1460) / 1460 = 37.2 ~= 37 => in case do it with 36
- ./run.sh --cca my_cca --size-gib 1 --rtt-ms 0.4 --rate-mbit 1000 --tbf-rate 1Gbit --tbf-burst 50000b  --tbf-limit 5840 --cwnd 36
- NOTE: for this experiment, <ins>we will disable the TSO (otherwise it will load ore than 3000 bytes at once to the TBF queue, causing the TBF queue to constantly requeue)</ins>... we tried sending 1GB without disabling TSO and it is taking tremendous amount of time... need to check whether this would effect the result
- `sudo ethtool -K eno1 tso off`
- `sudo ethtool -K eno1 gso off`
- client_server/DB/1GB/1GB_49346_36 (**342.28s**)
- client_server/DB/1GB/1GB_59160_0  (**285.02s**)

The performance of the optimal algorithm was way worse than TCP Reno. Thus, we will try with 1/2 the BDD size to see what happens (from the graph above, the performance doesn't seem to be too bad for this). The new optimal cwnd will be: (50000 + 25000 - 1460) / 1460 = 50.369 ~= 50 => in case do it with 49
- ./run.sh --cca my_cca --size-gib 1 --rtt-ms 0.4 --rate-mbit 1000 --tbf-rate 1Gbit --tbf-burst 50000b  --tbf-limit 25000 --cwnd 49
- /client_server/DB/1GB/1GB_52870_49 (**11.238548278808594s**)
- /client_server/DB/1GB/1GB_37326_0 (**9.724140644073486s**)

The optimal cwnd is still worse than the TCP Reno. => *Does this mean that the simulation was wrong?*
Since the average RTT for this setup is 0.3 ms, we will recalculate the BDD again. (0.0003 * 1,000,000,000) / 8 = 37500 bytes. (37500 + 25000 - 1460) / 1460 = 41.81 ~= 42 => in case do it with 41
- ./run.sh --cca my_cca --size-gib 1 --rtt-ms 0.3 --rate-mbit 1000 --tbf-rate 1Gbit --tbf-burst 50000b  --tbf-limit 25000 --cwnd 41
- client_server/DB/1GB/1GB_53558_41 (**15.097843885421753**)

It's actually slower...

## Testing with the case where queue_size > BDD, burst_size == 50000
We will try with the queue_size of 50000 bytes first. The new optimal cwnd will be: (50000 + 75000 - 1460) / 1460 = 84.616 ~= 85 => in case do it with 84
- ./run.sh --cca my_cca --size-gib 1 --rtt-ms 0.4 --rate-mbit 1000 --tbf-rate 1Gbit --tbf-burst 50000b  --tbf-limit 75000 --cwnd 84
- client_server/DB/1GB/1GB_54644_84 (**9.198986768722534**)
- client_server/DB/1GB/1GB_54864_85 (**9.141910314559937**)
- client_server/DB/1GB/1GB_47416_86 (**9.126748323440552**) <- optimal?
- client_server/DB/1GB/1GB_58224_87 (**9.130571365356445**)
- client_server/DB/1GB/1GB_56684_90 (**9.133797645568848**)
- client_server/DB/1GB/1GB_54220_0 (**9.188273906707764**)

It seems like the queue_size always has to be >= BDD in order for the optimal cwnd to achieve a better result. Another influencing factor might be the **token generation rate**. In the simulation, the link had a bandwidth of 200Mbps and token generation rate of 20Mbps. 

![alt text](research_images/bd_higher_than_tk_gen.png) 

In our case, the link bandwidth is 1GB and the token generation rate is 1GB as well. <ins>If the token generation rate and the link bandwidth are identical, the bucket will generally remain empty (or very near zero) while you are actively transmitting at full speed</ins>




